#lang rhombus/and_meta
// The deck's furniture: the three faces it is set in and the check that they are
// the ones being drawn with, the page a slide starts from, the words a bubble is
// written in, and the bands that say what is drawn over what.
//
// Everything here is about how the talk looks rather than about what it says, so
// the scheme is one edit and nothing downstream has an opinion about it.

import:
  lib("glide-pptx/runtime.rhm") open
  "slidehelpers.rhm" open
  "magicmove.rhm" open
  "write.rhm" open
  pict as pc
  pict/text as text
  draw

export:
  as_pict
  band_callout
  band_chip
  band_flight
  band_point
  band_rule
  band_wires
  blank_slide
  bubble_at
  code
  code_face
  code_font
  code_indent
  code_sub
  divider
  fill_width
  frame_box
  greek
  in_section
  keynote_blue
  math_face
  math_font
  math_text
  code_text
  prose_text
  box_dx
  box_dy
  media
  crop_pict
  robot
  robot_says
  node_font
  prose
  prose_face
  prose_font
  prose_stack
  put
  segs_pict
  segs_pieces
  slide_height
  slide_width
  small_prose
  staged
  titled
  title_text
  title_x
  title_y
  page_canvas
  with_icon

def slide_width = 1920.0
def slide_height = 1080.0

// ------------------------------------------------------------------------ faces
// The deck is set in three faces, and every run names one of these rather than a
// typeface, so the scheme is one edit and a fourth face cannot creep in unnamed:
//
//   `prose_font`  titles, captions, callouts -- anything read as English
//   `code_font`   the code boxes, and the e-graph node labels, which are the same
//                 program terms drawn as a graph
//   `math_font`   Greek, primes and the rewrite rules -- the notation, not the code
//
// `code_font` is the theme default too, so a run naming no typeface gets it. That
// default is why the Greek has to be said out loud: PT Mono has no Greek at all,
// so every unnamed sigma in this file was drawn by whatever racket/draw reached
// for instead -- Noto Sans on this machine, something else on a Mac. Naming the
// face is the difference between choosing one and being handed one.
def prose_font = "Helvetica Neue"
def code_font = "PT Mono"
def math_font = "Times New Roman"

// The e-graph node labels get their own name because which face they want is a
// judgement rather than a fact. They follow the notation: they sit beside the
// rewrite rules, they hold the same Greek and primes, and PT Mono draws `0` with a
// slash through it and gives `1` a flag, which is wrong for a term in a graph.
// Setting this to `code_font` draws them as the code boxes instead -- but then every
// sigma needs an exception, because PT Mono has no Greek at all.
def node_font = math_font

current_default_font(code_font)

// ------------------------------------------------------------------------ fonts
// racket/draw substitutes silently when a family is missing, and a substitute
// with different metrics is exactly what goes wrong here: line spacing in this
// runtime is a percentage of the *font size*, so the leading does not move when
// the face does -- only the substitute's ink height does. With Noto Sans
// standing in for Helvetica Neue, the 116pt title's two lines overlapped by
// 5.6pt and ran together.
//
// So name every family the deck asks for and refuse to run on the wrong one. A
// family may declare a stand-in, for the case where it cannot be installed at
// all (Helvetica Neue is Apple's); the stand-in has to be installed, and the
// name has to actually resolve to it, which is checked by measuring -- a font
// reports the face that was asked for, not the one that was drawn.
//
// `fonts` is a folder beside this file. Anything in it is loaded before a
// single glyph is measured, so a deck can carry the faces it is set in rather
// than hope for them: `raco glide fonts talk.rhm` puts them there.
register_fonts("fonts")
//
// One entry per face the deck is set in. Two entries used to be here that were not
// faces: Medium is a weight of the sans that Keynote wrote out as a family of its
// own, and the single Arial run was on an empty string. Both are gone rather than
// checked.
check_fonts(
  [code_font],
  [prose_font, "Nimbus Sans"],                // Helvetica clone, Helvetica metrics
  [math_font, "Tinos"]                        // metric-compatible with Times
)

// Images sit next to this file, so the program travels as a folder.
def media = media_lookup("media")

// The runtime builds Racket picts, but rhombus/pict's animation works on its own
// `Pict`. `Pict.from_handle` is glide-pptx's own bridge between the two -- its
// README calls it exactly that.
// A Racket pict from the runtime crosses over; a `Pict` is already across.
fun as_pict(q): if q is_a pc.Pict | q | pc.Pict.from_handle(q)

// A slide that was several: `base_canvas` is what shows before the first click,
// and each stage fades in over everything already on screen, one click apiece.
//
// `wire(p, k)` adds what is drawn *between* elements -- the edges of a graph --
// to the picture at stage `k`: to the base for `k = 0`, and for each later stage
// to a ghost of the base under the stage's own elements, so that an edge can join
// a node the stage adds to one that was there already, and fades in with it.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The talk's sections.
//
// `divider(k)` is the slide between sections: the bar full size in the middle of
// the slide, which then magic-moves up into the top margin. `in_section(k, ...)`
// puts that same bar, at that same size and place, above a content slide -- so the
// move ends exactly where the next slide's header begins and the bar appears to
// stay put across the cut.
//
// The header's discs sit around y 18..50 and the deck's slide titles start at
// y 64, which is what the disc and text scales in `header_layout` are chosen
// against. Retitle a slide higher than that and the two will meet.
def sections = ["Motivate Effectful E-Graphs", "Example Effectful E-Graph",
                "Effect-Safe Extraction", "It Works!"]

fun section_canvas():
  as_pict(slide_canvas(~width: slide_width, ~height: slide_height,
                       ~background: hex("FFFFFF")))

// The outline and the header are the one text on screen for the whole talk, and the
// only text that names no face: `slidehelpers` draws them with `current_font`, whose
// default is slideshow's rather than anything this deck declared or `check_fonts`
// checked. Both entry points are wrapped so that they are built in the prose face,
// which also picks up the numbers in the discs.
fun in_prose_face(mk):
  fun ():
    parameterize { text.current_font: text.current_font() with (name = prose_font) }:
      mk()

// `~shots` is one picture per section, for the outline to show beside the sections
// the talk has been through. They are built in `shots.rhm`, which the running order
// hands over -- this file is below the slides they are cut from and cannot reach
// them itself.
//
// A divider arrives from the side, where the slides within a section arrive from
// below: turning to the outline is turning a page, and the show reads which from
// the pict's own metadata (`transition_in`, in glide-pptx's `staged.rhm`).
//
// `~next` is the title of the slide that follows, which the press that puts the
// list away writes at the title's place -- so that press is one gesture, the
// outline going up and the next slide's title coming on, and the cut to the slide
// then changes nothing. The slide it names has to be one built with `titled`, with
// its title where `titled` puts it, and be the one that follows: the string is
// its title constant, exported by the slide's own file, so the two cannot drift.
fun divider(k :: Int, ~shots: shots :: maybe(List) = #false,
            ~next: next :: maybe(String) = #false):
  in_prose_face(
    fun ():
      def coming:
        if next
        | put(pc.blank(~width: slide_width, ~height: slide_height),
              title_text(next), title_x, title_y)
        | #false
      // only the first divider writes the list out; after that the list is familiar
      // and what matters is which disc is lit
      def p :~ pc.Pict = outline_to_header(sections, ~at: k, ~canvas: section_canvas(),
                                           ~intro: k == 0, ~shots: shots, ~next: coming)
      // with a title to write, the rise ends on the next slide's opening picture
      // and is the press that moves on: no held page, which the cut would repeat
      p.epoch_set_metadata(0, p.epoch_metadata(0) ++ { #'transition: #'right }
                                ++ (if next | { #'hold: #false } | {})))

// `~cut: #true` is for the first slide of a section: the divider it follows has
// just put the header where this slide's header is, so it arrives with a cut and
// the bar stays put. A pan would carry the bar off the top and bring the same bar
// back in from the bottom.
//
// `~hold: #false` is for a slide whose last press hands over to the next slide --
// it ends on the picture the next one opens with, and the next arrives with a cut
// -- so that last press is the one that moves on, rather than resting first on a
// page the cut would then repeat.
fun in_section(k :: Int, mk, ~cut: cut :: Boolean = #false, ~hold: hold :: Boolean = #true):
  in_prose_face(
    fun ():
      def p :~ pc.Pict = section_header(sections, ~at: k, ~canvas: as_pict(mk()))
      def marks:
        (if cut | { #'transition: #'none } | {}) ++ (if hold | {} | { #'hold: #false })
      if marks == {}
      | p
      | p.epoch_set_metadata(0, p.epoch_metadata(0) ++ marks))

fun staged(base_canvas, stages, ~wire: wire = fun (p, k): p):
  def base = wire(as_pict(base_canvas), 0)
  for values(p = base) (st in stages, k in 1..):
    def extra:
      wire(pc.overlay(~horiz: #'left, ~vert: #'top, base.ghost(), as_pict(st)), k)
    fade_in(p, extra, ~horiz: #'left, ~vert: #'top)

// Keynote's blue, which the state parts of the e-graph are drawn in: the state
// nodes' borders and the edges between them. See `chain_wire`.
def keynote_blue = draw.Color(4, 51, 255)

// Every word the deck draws goes through one of these three, so that it is text
// that writes itself when a press brings it on: `written_text` carries the glyph
// outlines its pen traces, and settles into exactly the text `pict.text` draws.
//
// `~arrive` says how prose comes on. Sentences -- what a bubble says, an equation
// -- are written, letter by letter. A label -- a big short word over a picture,
// read at a glance -- pops instead (`#'pop`), and a title fades (`#'fade`).
fun prose_text(s :: String, ~size: size :: Real = 40.0,
               ~bold: bold :: Boolean = #false, ~italic: italic :: Boolean = #false,
               ~color: color = "black",
               ~arrive: arrive = #'write) :: pc.Pict:
  def font = draw.Font(~name: prose_font, ~size: size,
                       ~weight: if bold | #'bold | #'normal,
                       ~style: if italic | #'italic | #'normal)
  match arrive
  | #'pop: popped(pc.text(s, ~font: font).colorize(as_color(color)))
  | #'fade: faded(pc.text(s, ~font: font).colorize(as_color(color)))
  | ~else: written_text(s, ~color: color, ~font: font)
fun code_text(s :: String, ~size: size :: Real = code_size,
              ~italic: italic :: Boolean = #false, ~color: color = "black") :: pc.Pict:
  written_text(s, ~color: color,
               ~font: draw.Font(~name: code_font, ~size: size,
                                ~style: if italic | #'italic | #'normal))
fun math_text(s :: String, ~size: size :: Real = code_size, ~color: color = "black") :: pc.Pict:
  written_text(s, ~color: color, ~font: draw.Font(~name: math_font, ~size: size))

// Where Keynote's text boxes put their ink: 4 across and 3 down from the corner of
// the box, measured against the deck's 48pt labels. A label that used to be a text
// box at (x, y) is text at (x + box_dx, y + box_dy).
def box_dx = 4.0
def box_dy = 3.0

// The size the code box is set at, which the rule under it is scaled to match.
def code_size = 40.0

def code_face = draw.Font(~name: code_font, ~size: code_size)
def math_face = draw.Font(~name: math_font, ~size: code_size)
fun code(s :: String) :: pc.Pict: code_text(s)
fun code_sub(s :: String) :: pc.Pict: code(s).scale(0.6)
// the Greek is set in the notation face, as everywhere else in the deck: PT Mono
// has none
fun greek(s :: String) :: pc.Pict: math_text(s)
def code_indent = code("  ").width    // the body, inside the signature

// A bordered box, drawn from its path so that a press can write it. `~rounded` is
// in OOXML's units, thousandths of a percent of the shorter side, as the shapes
// Keynote exported had it.
fun frame_box(w :: Real, h :: Real, ~rounded: r = #false) :: pc.Pict:
  def radius = if r | r / 100000.0 * math.min(w, h) | 0.0
  written_shape(rounded_path(w, h, radius), ~width: w, ~height: h,
                ~fill: "white", ~line: "black", ~line_width: 1.0)

// A line built from its pieces, in the order they are written. A piece with a role
// is one that stands for a node in the graph; `#false` is punctuation.
fun segs_pict(segs :~ List) :: pc.Pict:
  pc.beside(~vert: #'bottom,
            & List.append(& for List ([role, ps] in segs): ps))

// The named pieces of a line, as [role, pict, x offset from the line's left].
fun segs_pieces(segs :~ List) :: List:
  def values(out, _):
    for values(acc :~ List = [], dx :~ Real = 0.0) ([role, ps] in segs):
      def w: for values(t :~ Real = 0.0) (p :~ pc.Pict in ps): t + (p :~ pc.Pict).width
      values((if role == #false
              | acc
              | acc ++ [[role, pc.beside(~vert: #'bottom, & ps), dx]]),
             dx + w)
  out

// A pict at (x, y) on `base`, top-left corner; `p` may be one of the runtime's picts.
fun put(base :: pc.Pict, p, x :: Real, y :: Real) :: pc.Pict:
  pc.overlay(~horiz: #'left, ~vert: #'top, ~dx: x, ~dy: y, base, as_pict(p))

// A pict scaled to exactly the width it is given, up or down.
fun fill_width(p :: pc.Pict, w :: Real) :: pc.Pict: p.scale(w / p.width)

// A bubble pointing at a node, on a transparent layer of its own.
//
// Built once and handed to every press that shows it: a bubble rebuilt per press is
// a new pict each time, and a new pict fades in over the old one instead of staying
// put. What it points at is a laundered ghost of the node placed where that node
// goes -- the callout needs something to aim at, and a second copy of the node
// itself would be a second thing to find. `~body` is where the bubble's own corner
// lands; the spike is worked out from there.
fun bubble_at(node, ~spot: spot :~ List, ~body: body :~ List,
              ~color: color, ~content: content :: pc.Pict) :: pc.Pict:
  def marker = pc.blank(~width: 1.0, ~height: 1.0)
  def target = as_pict(node).launder().ghost()
  def probe:
    put(put(blank_slide, marker, body[0], body[1]), target, spot[0], spot[1])
  multi_callout(~on: probe, ~place: marker, ~at: [target],
                ~color: color, ~padding: 18.0, content)

// A word for a bubble that has to fit somewhere narrow.
fun small_prose(s :: String) :: pc.Pict: prose_text(s, ~size: 32.0, ~bold: #true)

// A sentence for a bubble, in the deck's Helvetica Neue: each item is a string
// and how it is set, `#'plain`, `#'bold`, `#'bold_italic` -- for the one word in a
// bubble that names a thing rather than says it -- or `#'sub`.
def prose_face = draw.Font(~name: prose_font, ~size: 40.0)
fun prose(items :: List) :: pc.Pict:
  pc.beside(~vert: #'bottom,
            & for List ([s, style] in items):
                match style
                | #'bold: prose_text(s, ~bold: #true)
                | #'bold_italic: prose_text(s, ~bold: #true, ~italic: #true)
                | #'sub: prose_text(s).scale(0.6)
                | ~else: prose_text(s))

// Several of those, stacked. A bubble beside the code box has the slide's right
// half to live in, and a sentence long enough to need more than that is broken
// across lines here rather than left to run off the edge.
fun prose_stack(rows :: List) :: pc.Pict:
  pc.stack(~sep: 4.0, ~horiz: #'left, & for List (r in rows): prose(r))

// The robot, from the 2022 explanations talk, saying something. It is the deck's
// one recurring character: it turns up when a moment is worth a face and nowhere
// else, and never two at once.
//
// The sizes are per face rather than one height for all of them, because the ones
// with something over their head -- a bulb, a question mark -- have it inside the
// picture, so matching the image heights would leave those two with small heads.
// These match the *heads*.
def robot_art:
  { #'idea: ["robot-idea.png", 135.6, 165.0],
    #'puzzled: ["robot-puzzled.png", 133.4, 150.0],
    #'thinking: ["robot-thinking.png", 150.0, 150.0],
    #'teeth: ["robot-teeth.png", 112.0, 112.0],
    #'smile: ["robot-smile.png", 122.9, 112.0],
    #'frown: ["robot-frown.png", 122.9, 112.0] }
fun robot(face) :: pc.Pict:
  def [file, w, h] = (robot_art :~ Map)[face] :~ List
  as_pict(image_pict(media(file), w, h))
// Beside the words by default. `~above` puts it over them instead, for a bubble
// that has to go somewhere narrow.
//
// `~scale` shrinks the *face*, never the words. A bubble that will not fit is a
// bubble with too big a robot in it or one in the wrong place; it is never a reason
// to set the deck's prose smaller than the deck sets it.
fun robot_says(face, content :: pc.Pict, ~sep: sep :: Real = 16.0,
               ~above: above :: Boolean = #false,
               ~scale: k :: Real = 1.0) :: pc.Pict:
  def r = robot(face).scale(k)
  if above
  | pc.stack(~sep: sep, ~horiz: #'center, r, content)
  | pc.beside(~sep: sep, ~vert: #'center, r, content)

// A window onto a pict: the pict drawn through a hole of its own, which is what
// lets the outline show the corner of a section that says what it was rather than
// the whole slide shrunk to a stamp nobody can read.
fun crop_pict(p :: pc.Pict, ~at: at :~ List, ~size: size :~ List) :: pc.Pict:
  def [x, y] = at
  def [w, h] = size
  pc.dc(fun (target :: draw.DC, dx, dy):
          target.save()
          def win = draw.Region()
          win.rectangle([dx, dy, w, h])
          target.clipping_region := win
          p.draw(target, ~dx: dx - (x :~ Real), ~dy: dy - (y :~ Real))
          target.restore(),
        ~width: w, ~height: h)

// A slide with the deck's title at the deck's place, and nothing else yet.
//
// The title is text rather than Keynote's text box, so that it is a thing the deck
// can write. `~box` is where Keynote's box was; the text lands where the box put
// its ink -- measured, on the box's 85pt bold: 4 across and 15 up from the corner
// -- give or take the letter-spacing Keynote took out. `~tag` is kept for Glide,
// which names a slide's title by it; `~nudge` is a correction to where it goes.
def title_face = draw.Font(~name: prose_font, ~size: 85.0, ~weight: #'bold)
def title_x = 95.0 + 4.0
def title_y = 85.0 - 15.0
// The title on its own, for a slide that changes its title while everything under
// it stays: a frame key. A title fades rather than writes -- it is read whole -- so
// a press that brings one on fades it up, and one that changes it cross-fades.
fun title_text(title :: String) :: pc.Pict: faded(pc.text(title, ~font: title_face))
// The white page every slide starts from.
fun page_canvas() :: pc.Pict:
  as_pict(slide_canvas(~width: slide_width, ~height: slide_height,
                       ~background: hex("FFFFFF")))
fun titled(title :: String, ~tag: tag :: String,
           ~nudge: nudge :: maybe(List) = #false,
           ~box: box :: List = [95.0, 85.0]) :: pc.Pict:
  def [nx, ny] = nudge || [0.0, 0.0]
  put(page_canvas(), title_text(title), box[0] + 4.0 + nx, box[1] - 15.0 + ny)

// Keynote's little "program becomes a tree" icon in the top-right corner.
fun with_icon(canvas :: pc.Pict) :: pc.Pict:
  fun box():
    shape_pict(~width: 23.528, ~height: 23.528,
               ~geom: preset_geom("roundRect", [pair("adj", "val 30640")]),
               ~fill: hex("FFFFFF"), ~line: make_stroke(hex("000000"), ~width: 3.0))
  def [b1, b2, b3, b4, b5, b6, b7] = for List (i in 0..7): box()
  // Each bar answers to a name of its own. The tag comes from the call, where
  // Glide can trace an editor correction back to this source location.
  fun bar(x, y, w, ~tag: tag :: String, ~nudge: nudge = #false):
    at(x, y, ~tag: tag, ~nudge: nudge,
       shape_pict(~width: w, ~height: 5.15, ~fill: hex("929292")))
  def icon:
    group_pict(~width: 327.683, ~height: 182.827,
               at(0.0, 62.869, ~name: "Group (2)",
                  group_pict(~width: 106.985, ~height: 66.994,
                             at(0.0, 0.0, ~name: "Rectangle",
                                shape_pict(~width: 106.985, ~height: 66.994, ~fill: hex("FFFFFF"),
                                           ~line: make_stroke(hex("000000"), ~width: 1.0))),
                             bar(4.646, 4.397, 60.549, ~tag: "Bar 1"),
                             bar(11.612, 11.976, 64.295, ~tag: "Bar 2"),
                             bar(11.612, 19.555, 79.846, ~tag: "Bar 3"),
                             bar(19.007, 27.133, 34.404, ~tag: "Bar 4"),
                             bar(11.612, 34.712, 49.795, ~tag: "Bar 5"),
                             bar(19.007, 42.29, 56.852, ~tag: "Bar 6"),
                             bar(11.612, 49.869, 47.325, ~tag: "Bar 7"),
                             bar(11.612, 57.447, 90.727, ~tag: "Bar 8"))),
               at(193.792, 0.0, ~name: "Group (3)",
                  group_pict(~width: 133.891, ~height: 182.827,
                             at(110.363, 159.299, ~name: "Rounded Rectangle", b1),
                             at(82.772, 107.433, ~name: "Rounded Rectangle (2)", b2),
                             at(55.182, 159.299, ~name: "Rounded Rectangle (3)", b3),
                             at(55.182, 53.717, ~name: "Rounded Rectangle (4)", b4),
                             at(32.239, 107.433, ~name: "Rounded Rectangle (5)", b5),
                             at(0.0, 159.299, ~name: "Rounded Rectangle (6)", b6),
                             at(55.182, 0.0, ~name: "Rounded Rectangle (7)", b7))),
               at(150.474, 85.484, ~name: "Arrow",
                  shape_pict(~width: 37.732, ~height: 21.764,
                             ~geom: preset_geom("rightArrow",
                                                [pair("adj1", "val 34890"), pair("adj2", "val 56320")]),
                             ~fill: hex("000000"))))
  arrows(~on: put(canvas, icon, 1476.341, 50.118),
         [[b2, b1], [b2, b3], [b5, b3], [b5, b6], [b7, b4], [b4, b1], [b4, b2], [b4, b5]],
         ~line_width: 2)

// A transparent slide, for a layer that goes over the page.
def blank_slide = pc.blank(~width: slide_width, ~height: slide_height)

// What is drawn over what, bottom to top:
//
//   band_rule    the rule box -- under the picture it is about, so a region or a
//                node that arrives while the rule is still up is drawn over it
//   band_below   the regions round the classes
//   band_nodes   the nodes
//   band_wires   the graph's own arrows, over the nodes and the regions, so an
//                arrowhead is never tucked behind a box and a dashed equivalence
//                inside a class is not washed out by it
//   band_point   the pointers a rule lays over the picture while it matches -- over
//                the rule too, so each one reads from the variable it starts at
//                rather than from the point where it leaves the box
//   band_chip    the chips laid over the rule's variables -- a band of their own,
//                since two things in one band have no order between them and a chip
//                behind the box it is laid on is invisible
//   band_callout the bubbles
//   band_flight  whatever is in flight, so a copy on its way from the rule into the
//                graph is never behind the box it left
def band_rule = band_below - 1
def band_wires = band_nodes + 1
def band_point = band_nodes + 2
def band_chip = band_nodes + 3
def band_callout = band_nodes + 4
def band_flight = band_nodes + 5
